SETUP
NETWORK CONFIGURATION
First I had to get all my virtual machines installed and ready before I could use them to practice. I set easily recognizable names for each VM so I could tell them apart if I'm connecting from one to the other. I then put them all on 1 virtual network, with the Server VM having a second NIC for NAT with the host, as well. This will improve security because it will allow traffic from client VMs to be isolated until it goes through the server. Finally, I also set up VMWare Tools on each machine, so I can copy & paste between my host and the VM, for example.

SERVER INITIALIZATION
After installing all of the OSs, I had to set up the Server VM with Active Directory, DHCP, and DNS so client systems can be added to a domain and traffic can be appropriately managed. Once the features are installed, promotion of the server to domain controller was necessary. This allows for centralized management and enforcement of security through managed access to resources.
While promoting to domain controller, I received an alert that the Admin password was weak. "net user Administrator [password]" fixes that. This is an important step because many admin accounts are high priority targets, and default Admin passwords can be found online. (I also then disabled that default Admin account, to confirm further that it could not be targeted.) Once that was complete, I configured a few accounts so I can have something to potentially attack (and discover) later.
There was a second alert that the server didn't have a static IP. A static IP is necessary for this server because all client traffic will flow through it. The client VMs need a definite way to reach the server as a dynamic IP could create issues with routing. Static IPs and subnet masks were configured to ensure reliable routing of traffic. There was also a requirement to set the gateway/preferred DNS to itself. For the DHCP configuration, I pointed it to my admin credentials (which I set up a new user for, rather than the default Admin account). Next, the scope was configured and the router IP was set to the static IP for the server.
Finally, I needed Routing and Direct Access so the server can properly route client traffic. NAT configuration was pointed to the externally-facing NIC. This ensures that traffic going through the server is then directed to the external NIC for routing to the outside world.

ADDING CLIENTS TO THE DOMAIN
I then added everything to the AD domain. In Windows, the DNS server must be set to the IP of the server machine. From there, the workstation can be added to the domain. This will allow more granular control of the account and computer from one centralized area - the server. Domain admin credentials were required to authorize the join.
Kali was next to add to the domain, and it requires more configuration to do so. First, updating and upgrading the system ensures I am working with the latest (and most secure) versions of packages installed. DNS was also configured to point to the domain controller. Then, several packages were installed to allow me to join Kali to the domain. For simplicity, here is a command that does all of them:
sudo apt -y install realmd libnss-sss libpam-sss sssd sssd-tools adcli samba-common-bin oddjob oddjob-mkhomedir packagekit
From there, ensure that the AD domain is discoverable and therefore usable on Kali:
sudo realm discover [domain]
And finally:
sudo realm join --user=[domain admin account] [domain]
Ubuntu will largely follow a similar process, and this will be used for our Wazuh server.

